WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.056
Andrew Carnegie was a man with a dream.

2
00:00:03.057 --> 00:00:08.947
He believed that every person should have an opportunity to learn, whether they are rich or poor.

3
00:00:08.948 --> 00:00:11.433
He made that dream come true.

4
00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:15.106
Andrew Carnegie was born in 1835 in Scotland.

5
00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:24.623
His father was a weaver, but after steam-powered looms were introduced, he wasn't able to earn enough money.

6
00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:29.505
So his family immigrated to the United States when Andrew was 13.

7
00:00:29.506 --> 00:00:33.816
The Carnegies settled in Pittsburgh, but they were still poor.

8
00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:37.558
Andrew had to start working at a young age in a textile mill.

9
00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.572
He worked very hard but only got paid a small amount of money.

10
00:00:41.573 --> 00:00:49.582
Nonetheless Andrew was determined to be a successful businessman one day.

11
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:58.096
After many different jobs, Andrew eventually found work as a messenger boy in a telegraph office earning $2.50 per week.

12
00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:01.261
This was double the pay of his previous jobs.

13
00:01:01.262 --> 00:01:05.868
He memorized street names and the names of the men he delivered messages to.

14
00:01:05.869 --> 00:01:11.614
This way, he was able to save time and make more friends.

15
00:01:11.615 --> 00:01:15.555
Andrew could not go to school, but he wanted to study.

16
00:01:15.556 --> 00:01:20.460
One day, one of his friends told him about a library that he could visit for free.

17
00:01:20.461 --> 00:01:24.996
The library belonged to a rich man named Colonel James Anderson.

18
00:01:24.997 --> 00:01:31.191
The colonel was a kind man who allowed any working boy to borrow books from his library.

19
00:01:31.192 --> 00:01:35.699
Andrew spent as much time as he could reading the colonel's books.

20
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.448
Andrew was grateful to the colonel for letting him use his library.

21
00:01:39.449 --> 00:01:42.223
He never forgot the colonel's generosity.

22
00:01:42.224 --> 00:01:47.084
When Andrew was 18 years old, he began to work for the Pennsylvania Railroad.

23
00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:53.989
Andrew was the personal assistant to one of the managers, a man named Thomas Scott.

24
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:59.663
Mr. Scott liked Andrew because he was smart, quick, and very eager to learn.

25
00:01:59.664 --> 00:02:02.762
Mr. Scott taught Andrew about the railroad industry.

26
00:02:02.763 --> 00:02:05.136
He explained how companies make money.

27
00:02:05.137 --> 00:02:09.895
Andrew learned many things from Mr. Scott.

28
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:13.290
When Andrew was twenty, his father passed away.

29
00:02:13.291 --> 00:02:21.578
Andrew's father had not lived a successful life in America, but he always believed in helping those who were poor and less fortunate.

30
00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:26.204
This belief would remain with Andrew for the rest of his life.

31
00:02:26.205 --> 00:02:30.915
Soon Andrew began to use the information that Mr. Scott had taught him.

32
00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:36.362
Slowly but surely, Andrew was becoming the successful businessman he had dreamed of.

33
00:02:36.363 --> 00:02:43.846
In 1859 Carnegie became the superintendent of the Pennsylvania Railroad's western division.

34
00:02:43.847 --> 00:02:46.907
Much had changed in the life of Andrew Carnegie.

35
00:02:46.908 --> 00:02:50.251
Now he was a powerful and wealthy businessman.

36
00:02:50.252 --> 00:02:57.159
But some things never change. Andrew's love of reading remained the same as it was when he was a little boy.

37
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:02.868
"It would be wonderful if all children could have their own library," Andrew thought to himself.

38
00:03:02.869 --> 00:03:09.605
"But how could that be possible?"

39
00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:15.639
In 1901, at the age of 66, Andrew Carnegie decided to retire from work.

40
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:22.676
By selling his company to J. P. Morgan for $480 million, he became the richest man in the world.

41
00:03:22.677 --> 00:03:26.646
Now it was time for Andrew to pursue his other dreams.

42
00:03:26.647 --> 00:03:28.972
Andrew became a philanthropist.

43
00:03:28.973 --> 00:03:34.943
A philanthropist is a person who gives away his money to help others.

44
00:03:34.944 --> 00:03:40.694
Andrew decided he would use most of his wealth for something very important: education.

45
00:03:40.695 --> 00:03:44.993
Andrew remembered Colonel Anderson and the kindness he had shown him.

46
00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:48.476
He wanted to show that same kindness to all children.

47
00:03:48.477 --> 00:03:58.072
He decided to build a public library, a place where children, rich or poor, could come to read books for free.

48
00:03:58.073 --> 00:04:08.137
During his lifetime, Andrew Carnegie built over 2,500 public libraries, mostly in the United States but also in other parts of the world.

49
00:04:08.138 --> 00:04:12.922
Andrew Carnegie is remembered for all of the wonderful ways he gave back to society.

50
00:04:12.923 --> 00:04:21.962
By 1911, ten years after he retired, he had given away almost 90 percent of the money he had earned.

51
00:04:21.963 --> 00:04:28.220
He was not only the richest man in monetary wealth but also in the generosity he showed others.

52
00:04:28.221 --> 00:04:35.166
Andrew Carnegie understood that true happiness comes from giving.

